    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d45">

                <pb facs="0127" n="71" xml:id="pg71"/>

                <head>No. 45.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Seward to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1876-07-10"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 10, 1876</date>.
                        (Received September 5.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 106.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Woosung Railway was opened on the 30th
                    ultimo, between Shanghai and Kungwang, the half-way station.</p>
                <p>The exercises were not imposing, but it seems worth while to transmit to you the
                    newspaper account of them, so that it may be on record.</p>
                <p>On the 1st instant trains were run, upon which Chinese were given free passages.
                    A second newspaper slip inclosed will show you how they availed themselves of
                    the opportunity.</p>
                <p>The government does not appear disposed to take any further notice of the
                    enterprise.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEO. F. SEWARD.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d45-a1.xml" xpointer="d45-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
